<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maria Komnene, Queen of Jerusalem</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1154–c. 1217</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Maria Comnena · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komnenian-dynasty">Komnenian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maria Komnene was a Byzantine princess, great-niece of Emperor Manuel I, who married King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem">Amalric I of Jerusalem</a> in 1167. She was the mother of Queen Isabella I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. After Amalric's death in 1174 she married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balian-of-ibelin">Balian of Ibelin</a> and remained an influential figure in the politics of the crusader kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maria-komnene-queen-of-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Maria Komnene, Queen of Jerusalem" width="152" height="189" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MarieKomnene.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maria Komnene was the daughter of the Byzantine protosebastos John Doukas Komnenos and a grandniece of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a>. Her cousin Theodora Komnene had been queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> as the wife of Baldwin III, in a marriage that sealed his alliance with Manuel. Baldwin died childless in 1163, and his brother Amalric succeeded. To restore the Byzantine alliance, Amalric sent envoys to Manuel in 1165, and after two years of negotiation Maria was chosen.</p><p>She landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> in August 1167 and was crowned before her wedding, as Theodora had been. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> patriarch Amalric of Nesle married the couple at the Cathedral of Tyre on 29 August 1167. As queen she had no influence on government. Their only surviving child, Isabella, was born in 1172.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Amalric died on 11 July 1174, having granted Maria the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a> as her dower. His son Baldwin IV, who had a living mother, Agnes of Courtenay, succeeded, so Maria had no role at court and retired to Nablus with Isabella. When Baldwin proved to have leprosy and could not father an heir, Isabella and her half-sister Sibylla became crucial, and Maria became more powerful as Isabella's mother than she had been as Amalric's wife. In late 1177 she married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balian-of-ibelin">Balian of Ibelin</a>; they had four children, Helvis, John, Margaret and Philip.</p><p>From 1180 she belonged to the faction opposing Sibylla and her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a>. Historians believe she probably conducted the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> attacked it in September 1184, taking the whole population into the citadel so that no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> were killed.</p><p>After Baldwin V died in 1186, Maria and her party planned to crown Isabella and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-iv-of-toron">Humphrey IV of Toron</a>, but Humphrey submitted to Sibylla and Guy. Saladin won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> on 4 July 1187 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> on 2 October. When Sibylla died in 1190, Maria had Isabella's marriage to Humphrey annulled, over Isabella's reluctance, so that she could marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-of-montferrat">Conrad of Montferrat</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Balian died in 1194 and Maria did not remarry. She supervised the marriage contract of her granddaughter Alice of Champagne in 1210, and died in mid-1217 in the reign of her great-granddaughter Isabella II. All the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and much of the thirteenth-century nobility of both kingdoms descended from her. The hostile Itinerarium Regis Ricardi called her steeped in Greek filth from the cradle, while the historian Bernard Hamilton judged her resilient and adaptable, and concluded that there was some truth in her opponents' view of her as ruthless and scheming.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maria Komnene, Queen of Jerusalem” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Barisan's sons Hugh, Baldwin and Balian of Ibelin were leading barons; Balian married the dowager queen <b>Maria Komnene</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-ibelin">Lordship of Ibelin</a></li></ul></section>
